let __v_2 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mince\",\"minced beef\",\"ground beef\",\"beef mince\"]},\"Brown a packet of mince with a chopped onion, then add a tin of tomatoes and a stock cube: 20 minutes gives bolognese, or add kidney beans, cumin and chilli powder for chilli. Top it with mashed potato and grill it if you want a cottage pie.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"leftover chicken\",\"left over chicken\",\"cooked chicken\",\"already cooked chicken\",\"leftover turkey\",\"cooked turkey\"]},\"Cooked chicken needs no cooking: fold it into a curry, risotto or soup for the last two minutes, layer it in a pie under pastry, or use it cold in a salad with mayonnaise, lemon and something crunchy. Reheat it once, until piping hot all the way through.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chicken\",\"chicken breast\",\"chicken thighs\"]},\"Cut chicken into strips and fry it with an onion, then add a tin of tomatoes or a jar of curry sauce: rice or pasta turns either into dinner in about 20 minutes. Thighs are more forgiving than breast if you are new to it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"egg\",\"eggs\"]},\"Eggs are a whole meal: an omelette with whatever cheese and vegetables you have, scrambled eggs on toast, or a quick frittata finished under the grill. All three take under 15 minutes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"potato\",\"potatoes\"]},\"Boil diced potatoes for 12 minutes, then fry them with onion and any meat or veg for a quick hash, or top baked ones with beans and cheese. Chop them small so they cook in the time the rest does.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pasta\",\"spaghetti\",\"penne\",\"noodles\"]},\"Boil the pasta, and while it cooks soften garlic in olive oil, add a tin of tomatoes and a pinch of chilli: sauce and pasta are ready together in 12 minutes. A splash of the pasta water makes the sauce cling.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rice\"]},\"Cook the rice, then fry it with an egg, frozen veg and soy sauce: about 10 minutes from cold rice to egg fried rice. Or stir a tin of beans and tomatoes through it for a one-pan dinner.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tomato\",\"tomatoes\",\"tinned tomatoes\",\"chopped tomatoes\"]},\"A tin of tomatoes is a sauce: soften onion and garlic, add the tomatoes and simmer 10 minutes, then use it on pasta, with beans, or over chicken. A pinch of sugar fixes a tin that tastes sharp.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chickpea\",\"chickpeas\",\"beans\",\"lentils\"]},\"Fry onion and garlic, add a tin of chickpeas, beans or lentils with tomatoes and cumin, and simmer 15 minutes: a filling dinner with rice or bread. Lentils cook in the same time as rice, so they are the cheapest way to make a meal bigger.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cheese\"]},\"Cheese turns most things into dinner: grate it over pasta with a splash of the cooking water, toast it on bread with tomatoes, or melt it into an omelette. A grilled cheese on toast with a fried egg on top is a proper quick tea.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tuna\"]},\"Tuna, pasta and a tin of tomatoes make a 15-minute dinner, or mix tuna with mayonnaise, sweetcorn and pasta for a cold lunch. Add the tuna at the end so it stays in flakes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sausage\",\"sausages\"]},\"Fry or roast the sausages while potatoes boil, then mash them with butter for bangers and mash; any leftovers go into a pasta bake. Leave them whole and cook them gently, because a pricked sausage leaks its fat and splits anyway.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"fish\",\"salmon\",\"cod\"]},\"Wrap the fish in foil with lemon and butter and bake at 200C (180C fan) for 12 to 15 minutes, with rice or potatoes on the side. It is done as soon as the flesh flakes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bread\",\"toast\"]},\"Bread is dinner if you toast it: beans on toast, a fried-egg sandwich, or a cheese toastie with a tin of soup. A grated-cheese-and-onion toastie takes 5 minutes in a dry pan.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mushroom\",\"mushrooms\"]},\"Fry mushrooms hard until golden, then add cream or creme fraiche and serve them on toast or with pasta. Do not crowd the pan or they steam instead of browning.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vegetables\",\"veg\",\"broccoli\",\"carrots\"]},\"Roast chopped veg at 200C (180C fan) for 25 minutes with oil and salt, then stir it through pasta or rice; a fried egg on top makes it a meal. Frozen veg works too, straight into the pan for the last 5 minutes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bacon\"]},\"Bacon makes a quick pasta: fry it with an onion, add a tin of tomatoes or a splash of cream, and toss it with pasta. The fat in the pan is the flavour, so do not pour it away.\"]}]}")?;
frame.store("MEAL_IDEAS", __v_2.clone())?;
let __v_3 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"roast chicken\",\"roast dinner\",\"roasting chicken\",\"turkey\",\"whole chicken\"]},\"Roast potatoes in the same oven for the last 45 minutes (boil them 10 minutes first, drain and shake them in hot oil and salt), and peas or greens with butter. Gravy from the tin is fine. If there is room, a tray of carrots, parsnips and onion can go in beside the bird, so the whole dinner comes out of one oven.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sausages\",\"sausage\",\"bangers\"]},\"Mash and onion gravy: boil the potatoes 15 minutes, mash them with butter and milk, and soften sliced onion in the sausage pan with a spoon of flour and a splash of stock for the gravy. Peas take 3 minutes, and the pan the sausages cooked in is the flavour.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lamb\",\"lamb chops\",\"roast lamb\"]},\"New potatoes with butter and mint, peas, and yoghurt stirred with chopped mint and lemon instead of mint sauce. Roast carrots and parsnips if the oven is on anyway; both take about 40 minutes beside the meat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"steak\",\"beef\",\"roast beef\"]},\"Chips and a sharp green salad, or a jacket potato if the oven is already on. Cook the steak last and rest it 5 minutes, then dress the salad in the pan with oil, vinegar and a spoon of mustard so nothing in it is wasted.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"salmon\",\"fish\",\"cod\",\"haddock\",\"fishcakes\",\"fish pie\"]},\"New potatoes and peas, or rice with greens wilted in a pan with butter. A squeeze of lemon and yoghurt with chopped dill is all the sauce it needs, and both take less time than the fish.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"curry\",\"chilli\",\"daal\",\"dal\",\"dhal\"]},\"Rice, and naan or a chapati if you have them. Grate cucumber, salt it for 10 minutes and squeeze it dry, then mix with yoghurt and cumin for a raita that cools the heat; mango chutney and coriander if they are in the cupboard.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pasta\",\"spaghetti\",\"lasagne\",\"lasagna\"]},\"Garlic bread and a green salad with a sharp dressing, because the salad is what cuts through the sauce. Tear the bread, rub it with garlic, oil and salt and give it 8 minutes in a hot oven while the pasta cooks.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"burger\",\"burgers\",\"hot dogs\",\"barbecue\",\"bbq\"]},\"Coleslaw, chips and pickles: shred cabbage and carrot, salt it, squeeze it dry and mix it with mayonnaise and a splash of vinegar. Corn on the cob and a bowl of potato salad fill the same table without any more cooking.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"shepherds pie\",\"shepherd's pie\",\"cottage pie\",\"pie\",\"stew\",\"casserole\"]},\"Peas or greens with butter, and bread and butter to mop the plate. A spoon of mustard or horseradish on the side cuts through a rich pie, and both take 5 minutes from frozen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"soup\",\"broth\"]},\"Bread and butter, or a cheese toastie cut in fingers for dipping. Something crunchy on top - toasted seeds, croutons, a handful of crisps - turns a bowl of soup into a meal.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"risotto\",\"paella\",\"pilaf\"]},\"A green salad with lemon in the dressing, and bread. Keep the salad cold and sharp, since the rice is rich and soft, and put it on the table at the same time as the pan.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chips\",\"fries\",\"french fries\"]},\"Fish fingers, a fried egg or a burger, and something sharp with them: peas, pickles or coleslaw, or a chip butty with white bread, butter and vinegar.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ham\",\"gammon\",\"pork\"]},\"New potatoes, peas, and mustard or pickles: a spoon of mustard in the juices in the pan makes the sauce, and cabbage braised in butter fills the plate for very little.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rice\",\"pilau\",\"basmati\"]},\"Rice is the blank part of the plate, so it wants something wet and sharp with it: a curry, a daal, a chilli or a stir-fry, or grilled chicken with soy, ginger and lime. A spoon of yoghurt with mint or a squeeze of lemon on the rice itself makes the plainest meal taste finished.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mash\",\"mashed potato\",\"baked potato\",\"jacket potato\",\"potato\",\"potatoes\",\"new potatoes\",\"boiled potatoes\",\"chips\"]},\"Potatoes want something with juice on it: sausages and onion gravy, a pie, a stew, or a roast with the meat juices poured over. Peas, greens or a sharp pickle alongside stop it tasting flat, and for mash warm the plates and beat in butter and warm milk at the end.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chicken\",\"chicken thighs\",\"chicken breast\"]},\"Roast potatoes or mash, and something green with butter, plus a sauce made in the pan: splash in stock or wine, scrape the brown bits up and let it reduce for 2 minutes. That is a whole dinner from what is already on the hob.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"halloumi\",\"halloumi cheese\"]},\"Halloumi is salty and squeaky, so it wants something juicy and sharp: tomatoes with lemon and herbs, couscous with chopped apricots, or a flatbread with yoghurt and a little chilli. Fry it dry in a hot pan for a minute a side and eat it straight away, while the middle is still soft.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"feta\",\"feta cheese\"]},\"Feta is salty and crumbly, so it wants something sweet or juicy against it: watermelon, roasted peppers, tomatoes and cucumber with lemon and oregano, or a tray of roasted veg and chickpeas with feta crumbled over at the end. It does not melt, so add it last.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"corn on the cob\",\"sweetcorn\",\"corn\"]},\"Butter, salt and pepper, and then the wet, sharp things next to it: coleslaw, a tomato and red onion salad with lime, or a spoon of chilli butter melted over the cobs. It sits beside a barbecue, a roast chicken or a bowl of chilli without any more cooking, and 2 minutes under a hot grill after boiling blisters the kernels.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"prawns\",\"prawn\",\"king prawns\",\"shrimp\",\"prawn linguine\"]},\"Prawns take two minutes, so the rest is what you make first: garlic, chilli and butter in a pan with parsley, tossed through pasta or piled on toast, and a green salad or lemony courgette ribbons beside it. Bread to mop the pan is the best part.\"]}]}")?;
frame.store("SIDES", __v_3.clone())?;
let __v_4 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"roast lamb\",\"leg of lamb\",\"lamb chops\",\"lamb\",\"mutton\"]},\"Rosemary, garlic and black pepper are the classic on lamb: push slivers of garlic and rosemary into a leg, or rub chops with oil, salt, crushed garlic and a pinch of cumin. Anchovy mashed into the rub, or a spoon of mint sauce stirred through yoghurt, is the other one that works. Salt the meat the night before if you have the time.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"roast beef\",\"steak\",\"beef\",\"brisket\"]},\"Beef wants salt and coarse black pepper, and not much else: salt a steak 40 minutes before it goes in the pan, or rub a roast with mustard, thyme and horseradish before it goes in the oven. A spoon of horseradish in creme fraiche is the sauce, not the seasoning.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"roast chicken\",\"whole chicken\",\"chicken thighs\",\"chicken breasts\",\"chicken\",\"turkey\"]},\"Lemon, garlic, thyme and paprika: rub the skin with oil, salt, lemon zest, crushed garlic and a spoon of paprika, and put half a lemon inside. For pieces, the same mix with cumin instead of paprika is what most traybakes want.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pork\",\"pork chops\",\"belly pork\",\"gammon\",\"ham\"]},\"Fennel seed, sage and black pepper are made for pork: crush the fennel, mix it with salt and rub it into the fat, and put sage leaves under the skin. A little mustard or cider in the pan at the end finishes it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"salmon\",\"fish\",\"cod\",\"haddock\",\"trout\"]},\"Fish takes lemon, dill and butter, and salt before it cooks rather than after: zest the lemon over it, lay dill on top and put a knob of butter on each piece. Paprika or fennel seed for a fillet that will be fried hard.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tofu\",\"firm tofu\",\"silken tofu\",\"tempeh\"]},\"Tofu has no salt or fat of its own, so it needs both: soy sauce, sesame oil, garlic and ginger, or a marinade it sits in for half an hour after being pressed. Fry it hard in a little oil first, until the edges are brown, and sauce it after - added wet, it steams and stays pale.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"halloumi\",\"feta\"]},\"Chilli, lemon and oregano, with salt only if the cheese needs it: halloumi and feta are already salty, so they want heat and acid instead. Tofu wants soy, garlic and ginger before it goes in the pan, and a little cornflour to help it brown.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ribs\",\"rack of ribs\",\"pork ribs\",\"spare ribs\",\"baby back ribs\",\"beef ribs\"]},\"Ribs want sugar in the rub and acid in the sauce: brown sugar, paprika, salt, black pepper, garlic powder and a little cumin, rubbed on the night before or an hour ahead. Cook them low, covered, for two to three hours at 150C, then paint with barbecue sauce and finish them hot for ten minutes. The sugar is what makes the crust, and it burns above 200C, so sauce goes on late.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"potato\",\"potatoes\",\"new potatoes\",\"roast potatoes\",\"chips\"]},\"Salt, oil and a herb that can take the heat: rosemary or thyme in the tray with the potatoes, and salt in the water they boil in, not only at the end. Paprika or garlic granules dusted on halfway through browning.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rice\",\"pilau\",\"basmati\",\"couscous\"]},\"Rice wants salt in the water, a bay leaf or a cardamom pod while it cooks, and butter or oil stirred through at the end. Cumin seeds fried in the oil first is what turns plain rice into pilau.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tomatoes\",\"tomato\",\"cherry tomatoes\",\"passata\"]},\"Tomatoes want salt, a little sugar if they are pale, and a herb that is not shy: basil torn in at the end for a salad, oregano or thyme in the pan for a sauce, and garlic cooked gently rather than browned. Salt them ten minutes before they go in and the juices come out and turn into the sauce.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vegetables\",\"veg\",\"squash\",\"pumpkin\",\"butternut\",\"courgette\",\"courgettes\",\"carrots\",\"broccoli\",\"cauliflower\",\"aubergine\"]},\"Roast veg wants salt, oil and heat, then something sharp at the end: lemon zest, a splash of vinegar or a crumble of feta. Cumin and paprika for squash and carrots, oregano and chilli for courgette and aubergine, and salt halfway through rather than only at the start.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"burger\",\"burgers\",\"sausage meat\",\"meatballs\",\"mince\"]},\"Season the meat before it is shaped, not after: salt, black pepper and a little onion or mustard in the mix, then fry a spoonful to taste before you commit the lot. A burger needs nothing on the outside but salt, pepper and a hot pan.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"roast\",\"roast dinner\",\"roasting joint\",\"joint of meat\"]},\"Salt the joint the night before if you can, then rub it with oil and pepper before it goes in: lemon, garlic and thyme for chicken, mustard and horseradish for beef, rosemary and garlic for lamb, fennel and sage for pork. Salt early is the one that makes the difference, because it gets into the meat instead of sitting on the skin.\"]}]}")?;
frame.store("SEASONINGS", __v_4.clone())?;
let __v_5 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"Bring something with protein and fibre in it - nuts, fruit, yoghurt, oatcakes, cheese - and keep it in your drawer or the office fridge, so the 3pm vending machine is not the decision. It is cheaper too: a £1.20 snack a day is about £250 a year. Water in a bottle on the desk covers most of what people mistake for hunger.\",\"Bring something with protein and fibre in it - fruit, yoghurt, oatcakes, cheese, hummus - and keep it in your drawer or the office fridge, so the 3pm vending machine is not the decision. It is cheaper too: a £1.20 snack a day is about £250 a year. Water in a bottle on the desk covers most of what people mistake for hunger.\"],[\"Chestnuts: cooked ones crumble into a sprout and bacon dish, or simmer 15 minutes in milk and blitz into soup, which is sweet enough without cream.\",\"Chestnuts: simmer them 15 minutes and peel them while they are still hot, then crumble them into a stuffing or stir them through a soup - they mash like potato, and a chestnut soup is sweet enough on its own without any sugar. Roast them at 200C (180C fan) for 20 minutes if you want them to peel easily.\"],[\"Mash it: cubed swede boiled 20 minutes, drained well and mashed with butter, salt and plenty of black pepper, alongside sausages.\",\"Mash it: cubed swede boiled 20 minutes, drained well and mashed with butter, salt and plenty of black pepper. It roasts well too - cubes tossed in oil, 40 minutes at 200C (180C fan) until the edges caramelise - and it is a good soup with carrot and a little nutmeg.\"],[\"Shredded cabbage fried hot for 7 minutes with chopped bacon or smoked sausage, then stirred through pasta or mash, is a dinner rather than a side.\",\"Shredded cabbage fried hot for 7 minutes with garlic and a pinch of chilli, then stirred through mash or rice with a squeeze of lemon, is a dinner rather than a side. Add a spoon of mustard to the pan and it tastes of more than it is.\"],[\"A roast is one joint, potatoes, two veg and gravy, and the veg is where you ring the changes: carrots and parsnips roasted round the meat for the last 45 minutes, greens or cabbage steamed, and peas with mint if it is lamb. Add Yorkshire puddings for beef, stuffing and apple sauce for pork, bread sauce for chicken or turkey. Work back from the meat, because the joint rests 20 minutes and that is when the potatoes go up to full heat.\",\"A roast is potatoes, two veg and gravy, and the veg is where you ring the changes: carrots and parsnips roasted in oil for the last 45 minutes, greens or cabbage steamed, and peas with mint. The middle of the plate is a nut roast, a whole roasted cauliflower or a mushroom and lentil loaf, and the gravy is made with vegetable stock and a spoon of soy. Put the potatoes in at full heat 40 minutes before you want to eat and the timing looks after itself.\"],[\"Protein is a building material made of chains of amino acids: the body uses it to build and repair muscle, skin, hair and organs, and enzymes are proteins too. Meat, fish, eggs, milk, beans, lentils, nuts and tofu are good sources.\",\"Protein is a building material made of chains of amino acids: the body uses it to build and repair muscle, skin, hair and organs, and enzymes are proteins too. Beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, nuts, seeds and whole grains are good sources, and you do not have to combine them at one meal: a day of them does the job. Eggs and dairy count too if you eat them.\"],[\"Courgette pasta: sliced and fried with garlic in plenty of olive oil for 8 minutes, tossed with pasta and parmesan.\",\"Courgette: sliced and fried with garlic in plenty of olive oil for 8 minutes, until the edges brown, then dressed with lemon and plenty of black pepper - good on its own, through rice, or under a roast.\"]]}")?;
frame.store("DIET_VARIANTS", __v_5.clone())?;
let __v_6 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"milk\"]},\"Yes: freeze it fresh, leave an inch of room for it to expand, and shake it after thawing - it is fine for cooking and cereal even if it looks a little separated.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ice cream\",\"icecream\",\"ice-cream\",\"gelato\"]},\"Ice cream is already frozen, so it keeps months in its tub: press cling film onto the surface once it is open so it does not go icy. Home-made ice cream without a churn freezes hard, so stir it every half hour for the first two hours and it stays scoopable.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cream\"]},\"Cream is the one thing not to freeze: it splits and thaws out grainy. Freeze the cake or sponge plain and put fresh cream on when you serve it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cheese\"]},\"Grate hard cheese and freeze it in bags, then use it straight from frozen in cooking. Soft cheese and cream cheese go grainy, so keep those in the fridge.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"butter\"]},\"Yes: wrap it well, cut into portions first if you can, and it keeps about 3 months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"banana\",\"bananas\"]},\"Peel and slice them first, then bag them. They thaw soft, so they are for smoothies and banana bread rather than eating.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"egg\",\"eggs\"]},\"Not in the shell: beat the yolks and whites separately (or beat them whole), write how many on the bag, and use within 3 months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"avocado\",\"avocados\"]},\"Only as mash, or halves with lemon on them. It thaws soft, so it is for guacamole rather than salad.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"herb\",\"herbs\",\"basil\",\"parsley\",\"coriander\",\"cilantro\",\"mint\"]},\"Chop them and freeze in an ice-cube tray topped up with oil or water, or freeze whole sprigs in a bag for cooking.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"berry\",\"berries\",\"strawberry\",\"strawberries\",\"raspberry\",\"raspberries\",\"blueberry\",\"blueberries\"]},\"Spread them out on a tray first so they freeze loose, then bag them. They thaw soft, which is fine for crumble or yoghurt.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bread\",\"loaf\"]},\"Slice it first, then it goes straight in the toaster, and it keeps about 3 months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"stock\",\"gravy\"]},\"Cool it fast, freeze it flat in bags or in ice-cube trays, and it keeps about 3 months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cake batter\",\"batter\",\"sponge mix\"]},\"Yes: freeze the batter in the tin, wrapped, and bake it from frozen with 15 to 20 minutes extra at the usual temperature, or freeze it in portions and thaw overnight in the fridge.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cake\"]},\"Wrap it well, in slices if that is how you will eat it, and it keeps about 3 months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"wine\"]},\"Freeze what is left in ice-cube trays and drop a cube into a stew or a sauce.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mushroom\",\"mushrooms\",\"chestnut mushrooms\"]},\"Cook them first: raw mushrooms go slimy when frozen. Fry them until the water has gone, cool them fast, then freeze in bags for about 3 months and use them from frozen in sauces.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pizza\",\"pizzas\",\"pizza dough\",\"dough\"]},\"Yes: a cooked pizza freezes for about 2 months, wrapped, and goes into a hot oven straight from frozen. Raw dough freezes after the first rise, in an oiled bag, and thaws overnight in the fridge.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mince\",\"beef\",\"lamb\",\"pork\",\"fish\",\"salmon\",\"bacon\",\"meat\",\"cod\",\"haddock\",\"trout\",\"mackerel\",\"tuna\",\"prawn\",\"prawns\",\"shellfish\",\"sardines\",\"sea bass\",\"monkfish\",\"hake\",\"kippers\"]},\"Yes, cooked or raw: freeze it the day you buy it, or within a day of cooking it, in portions, and use it within 3 months. Thaw it in the fridge overnight rather than on the counter, cook it right through, and do not refreeze meat that has already been thawed.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rhubarb\"]},\"Yes: cut it into lengths, bag it and freeze it raw for about 3 months - it goes soft as it thaws, which is exactly what a crumble wants. Stewed rhubarb freezes well too, and slightly under-sweetened, since it takes sugar when you use it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"beetroot\",\"beet\"]},\"Cook it first: roast or boil whole beetroot, rub the skins off, then slice and bag it, and it keeps about 3 months. Raw beetroot freezes hard and woody, so cook first; pickled beetroot freezes badly, because the vinegar soaks in and the slices go limp.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sweet potato\",\"sweet potatoes\",\"yam\",\"yams\"]},\"Cook it first: roast, mash or par-boil chunks, cool and bag, and it keeps about 3 months. Raw sweet potato goes watery and black, so it is the one root that wants cooking before the freezer - and mash freezes better than chunks.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tofu\",\"bean curd\"]},\"Yes: press the water out, cut it into cubes and freeze it in a bag. The texture turns spongy and it takes up sauce better afterwards, so it is a good thing to do with a block you will not use; it keeps about 3 months and can be cooked from frozen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"jam\",\"marmalade\",\"preserve\",\"preserves\"]},\"Yes, jam freezes: it keeps its colour and set better than in a cupboard, and a jar keeps about a year in the freezer. Leave an inch of room in the jar for it to expand, and thaw it in the fridge overnight; it will not go off in the freezer, so the date on it is about quality.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mayonnaise\",\"mayo\",\"salad cream\"]},\"Not well: mayonnaise splits when it freezes and thaws oily and grainy, so a jar in the fridge door is the right place for it. A sandwich made with it does not freeze well either - keep mayo for the day of eating - though a cooked dish with mayo in it freezes fine.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"nuts\",\"almonds\",\"walnuts\",\"cashews\",\"peanuts\",\"pistachios\",\"pecans\",\"hazelnuts\"]},\"Yes, and it is the best place for them: nuts go rancid in a warm cupboard in a few months, and frozen they keep about a year. Bag them, press the air out, and use them from frozen - they thaw in minutes and toast straight from the freezer.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sandwich\",\"sandwiches\",\"wrap\",\"wraps\"]},\"Only a plain one: meat, cheese or peanut butter sandwiches freeze fine for about a month if you leave out the salad, cucumber and tomato, which thaw to water. Spread the filling to the edges so the bread does not dry, wrap each one, and let it thaw in the fridge overnight or pack it frozen for lunch.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"prawn\",\"prawns\",\"shrimp\",\"shrimps\"]},\"Yes, raw or cooked: freeze them the day you buy them, in a bag with the air pressed out, and use within 3 months. Thaw them in the fridge overnight rather than in warm water, and do not refreeze them once thawed - cooked prawns go rubbery if you freeze them twice.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"dumpling\",\"dumplings\",\"gyoza\",\"potsticker\",\"potstickers\"]},\"Yes: freeze them raw on a tray so they stay separate, then bag them, and cook from frozen - they need about 3 minutes longer in the pan or steamer, and no thawing. They keep about 3 months, and a frozen dumpling goes straight into soup.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"melon\",\"watermelon\",\"cantaloupe\",\"honeydew\"]},\"Yes: cut it into chunks, spread them on a tray to freeze loose, then bag them - and eat them frozen or in a smoothie, because melon thaws limp and watery. It keeps about 3 months, and a melon that is already cut goes in the fridge first and the freezer within 3 days.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mango\",\"mangoes\",\"papaya\",\"pineapple\"]},\"Yes: cut it into chunks, freeze them on a tray so they stay loose, then bag them. They thaw soft, so they are for smoothies, yoghurt or a lassi rather than a fruit salad, and they keep about 3 months. Pineapple is the one that holds its shape best, and frozen mango is what most smoothie bars use.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"date\",\"dates\",\"raisins\",\"sultanas\",\"dried fruit\"]},\"Yes: dried fruit freezes well and comes back as it was - bag it and it keeps about a year, which is longer than the cupboard. It does not go hard, so you can chop dates straight from the freezer for a cake, and a bag that has gone dry revives in the freezer.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"peach\",\"peaches\",\"plum\",\"plums\",\"apricot\",\"apricots\",\"nectarine\",\"nectarines\"]},\"Best halved and stoned first, then frozen on a tray and bagged: they keep about 3 months and are for crumble, jam or a smoothie. The skins slip off as they thaw, so there is no need to peel them first, and a peach freezes better than it keeps in the fruit bowl.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cream cheese\",\"soft cheese\",\"mascarpone\",\"ricotta\",\"brie\",\"camembert\",\"feta\"]},\"Soft cheese freezes but thaws grainy and a bit watery, so it is for cooking - a baked cheesecake, a sauce, a pasta bake - rather than for a cracker. Wrap it well, freeze up to 2 months, and thaw it in the fridge; a whole brie or camembert is better baked from frozen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"oat milk\",\"soya milk\",\"soy milk\",\"almond milk\",\"coconut milk\",\"plant milk\"]},\"Yes: plant milk freezes, though it can separate, so shake it hard once it has thawed. Leave an inch of room in the bottle or carton for it to expand, and a tin of coconut milk freezes well in an ice-cube tray - a cube is about a tablespoon for a curry.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"breast milk\",\"expressed milk\",\"pumped milk\"]},\"Expressed breast milk keeps 5 days in the fridge at the back and 6 months in the freezer, in the amounts you will use, dated. Thaw it in the fridge overnight and use it within 24 hours, warm it in a jug of warm water rather than the microwave, and never refreeze it or add fresh milk to a thawed bottle.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"formula milk\",\"formula\",\"baby formula\"]},\"Not the made-up bottle: mix each feed fresh with boiled water left to cool to about 70C, and throw away whatever is left in the bottle. Ready-made formula keeps 24 hours in the fridge once it is opened, and the freezer is not the place for it - frozen and thawed formula separates and is not worth the risk for a baby.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sour cream\",\"creme fraiche\",\"creme fresh\"]},\"It splits when it freezes and thaws grainy and watery, so it is no good as a spoonful on a baked potato afterwards. It is fine in a dish that is going to be cooked - a stroganoff or a cake - so freeze it stirred into that rather than on its own.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"baby food\",\"puree\",\"purees\",\"pureed food\",\"weaning food\"]},\"Yes: spoon it into an ice-cube tray, freeze it, then pop the cubes into a bag - each cube is about an ounce, so a couple makes a portion. It keeps about a month, thaws in the fridge or in a bowl of warm water, must be heated right through and cooled before it is offered, and never goes back in the freezer once warmed.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"grape\",\"grapes\"]},\"Yes: wash and dry them, spread them on a tray to freeze loose, then bag them. They go hard and sorbet-like, so they are for eating frozen or for smoothies rather than a fruit salad, and they keep about 3 months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"apple\",\"apples\",\"pear\",\"pears\"]},\"Best cooked first: stewed or sliced apple freezes about 3 months in bags, and it goes into a crumble from frozen. Raw slices go brown and soft, so toss them in lemon juice and use them for baking rather than a salad.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lemon\",\"lemons\",\"lime\",\"limes\",\"orange\",\"oranges\",\"grapefruit\"]},\"Juice them first and freeze the juice in an ice-cube tray - a cube is about a tablespoon - and zest them into a small bag; both go straight into cooking from frozen and keep about 3 months. A whole citrus fruit freezes but thaws soft and dull, so use those for juice rather than slices.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"orange juice\",\"juice\",\"apple juice\"]},\"Yes: pour it into a bottle with an inch of room for it to expand, or freeze it in ice-cube trays for drinks, and it keeps about 4 to 6 months. It separates as it thaws, so shake it, and the vitamin C tails off the longer it sits, which is why a month-old cube is for flavour rather than for goodness.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cucumber\",\"cucumbers\"]},\"Not for salad: a cucumber is nearly all water, so the ice bursts it and it thaws limp and watery. It is fine cooked into a soup or a curry, or frozen as tzatziki, where the water does not matter.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"coleslaw\",\"slaw\",\"sauerkraut\"]},\"Freeze the shredded cabbage on its own and it keeps for cooking; dressed coleslaw does not freeze, because the mayonnaise splits and the cabbage goes limp and watery. So bag the dry cabbage and dress a fresh bowl when you want one.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"guacamole\",\"guac\",\"hummus dip\"]},\"It freezes, but it thaws a little watery and dull: press cling film onto the surface so it does not go brown, freeze it for up to a month, then stir it well and use it in a wrap or on toast rather than as a dip for guests. Lemon juice on top helps it stay green.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lettuce\",\"salad\",\"salad leaves\",\"rocket\",\"arugula\",\"watercress\"]},\"Not if you want it raw: a leaf is nearly all water, so the ice bursts its cells and it thaws limp and brown. It is fine cooked, so braise it or stir it into soup instead, and a bag that has already gone limp is better cooked than frozen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"spring onion\",\"spring onions\",\"scallion\",\"scallions\"]},\"Yes: chop them, bag them and freeze them, and they go straight into cooking from frozen for about 3 months. They lose their crunch, so they are no good raw afterwards, and the white parts freeze better than the green.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vegetable\",\"vegetables\",\"veg\",\"peas\",\"spinach\",\"broccoli\",\"carrots\",\"carrot\",\"green beans\",\"sweetcorn\",\"sweet corn\",\"corn\",\"cauliflower\",\"cabbage\",\"sprouts\",\"sprout\",\"kale\",\"parsnip\",\"parsnips\",\"pepper\",\"peppers\",\"leek\",\"leeks\",\"courgette\",\"courgettes\",\"zucchini\",\"turnip\",\"turnips\",\"swede\",\"runner beans\",\"mangetout\",\"sugar snap\",\"beans\",\"butternut\",\"pumpkin\"]},\"Yes: blanch them in boiling water for 2 minutes, cool them in cold water, dry them and bag them. They keep about 3 months and go into the pan from frozen; cooked leftover veg is fine too, though it comes out softer.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"raw potato\",\"raw potatoes\",\"potato\",\"potatoes\",\"new potatoes\",\"spuds\"]},\"Not raw: raw potato goes black and mushy in the freezer. Cook it first - roast potatoes, chips, mash or par-boiled - and it keeps about 3 months, with mash the one that thaws best. Par-boiled and roughed up in the pan first gives you roast potatoes straight from frozen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"onion\",\"onions\",\"shallot\",\"shallots\",\"spring onions\",\"celery\",\"ginger\",\"garlic\",\"chilli\",\"chillies\",\"chili\",\"chilies\"]},\"Chop them raw and bag them: onions, shallots, celery, ginger, garlic and chillies all freeze without blanching and keep about 3 months. Freeze them spread out on a tray first if you want them loose, and cook them from frozen - they soften rather than brown.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tomato\",\"tomatoes\",\"cherry tomatoes\",\"passata\"]},\"Yes, and no blanching needed: freeze them whole on a tray, or halve them and bag them. The skins split and slip off as they thaw, so they are for sauces, soups and stews rather than salads, and they keep about 3 months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pasta\",\"spaghetti\",\"penne\",\"fusilli\",\"macaroni\",\"noodles\",\"tagliatelle\"]},\"Yes: cook it a minute short of the packet, toss it with a little oil or sauce so it does not clump, and freeze it in portion bags. It comes back in minutes in boiling water or straight in a sauce.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"scone\",\"scones\",\"fruit scone\",\"cheese scone\"]},\"Bake them first: cool them completely on a rack, bag them and they keep about 2 months. Warm them from frozen for 8 to 10 minutes at 160C (140C fan) and they come back close to fresh - raw dough freezes too, cut into rounds, and bakes from frozen with 3 minutes extra.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pancake\",\"pancakes\",\"crepe\",\"crepes\"]},\"Yes: cool them, stack them with baking paper between so they do not stick, bag the stack and freeze it for about 2 months. They go straight into a hot pan, or the toaster, from frozen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"waffle\",\"waffles\"]},\"Yes: cool them on a rack so they do not steam soft, bag them, and they keep about 2 months. Give them 3 or 4 minutes in a hot oven or the toaster from frozen and they crisp up again.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"crumpet\",\"crumpets\"]},\"Yes, in the pack they came in or bagged, for about 2 months, and they toast from frozen with an extra minute or two.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pastry\",\"shortcrust\",\"shortcrust pastry\",\"pie crust\",\"pastry dough\"]},\"Raw is the way: flatten the pastry into a disc, wrap it and it keeps about 3 months, then thaw it overnight in the fridge and roll it cold. A baked pastry case freezes too but it softens, so give it 5 minutes in a hot oven to crisp before you fill it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"crumble\",\"fruit crumble\",\"crumble topping\"]},\"Freeze it before it is baked, not after: a baked crumble goes soggy as it thaws. Assemble it in the dish, wrap the whole thing, and bake it from frozen at the usual heat plus 15 minutes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"yoghurt\",\"yogurt\",\"greek yoghurt\",\"greek yogurt\"]},\"It freezes but it separates, so it thaws grainy and watery and is no good on its own. It is fine in a smoothie or stirred into a curry or naan dough, and frozen in ice-cube trays it makes a quick lassi.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chocolate\",\"milk chocolate\",\"dark chocolate\"]},\"It freezes, but the surface goes white and powdery as it thaws. Wrap it tightly, thaw it in the fridge, and use it for melting or baking rather than for eating.\"]}]}")?;
frame.store("FREEZE_FACTS", __v_6.clone())?;
